course: dictionary
lesson: 7705
title: Psoriasis: a skin condition
level: primary-5
stars: 3
explain: Psoriasis is a long-lasting skin condition. It causes red, itchy, scaly patches, often on the elbows, knees and scalp. «Psoriasis is when skin cells grow too fast and build up in thick scaly patches.» That sentence tells you what is happening under the skin.
explain: The body makes new skin cells too fast, so they pile up on the surface. That is why the patches look thick and scaly. Doctors sometimes call these patches «psoriasis patches».
explain: Psoriasis is not catching. You cannot get it by touching someone who has it. Creams and other treatments can help, but the condition can come and go. People with skin conditions may feel self-conscious, so being kind matters.
fact: Psoriasis is a long-lasting skin condition, which means it can stay for a long time.
fact: It causes red, itchy, scaly patches, often on the elbows, knees and scalp.
fact: It is not catching: you cannot catch it by touching someone who has it.
fact: Doctors give creams and other treatments that help, but it can come and go.
fact: People with skin conditions may feel self-conscious, so kindness matters.
exercise: Psoriasis is a skin condition that causes what? | *Red, itchy, scaly patches | Broken bones | Bright blue skin all over | Sore throats and coughs
exercise: «Psoriasis is when skin cells grow too fast and build up in thick scaly patches.» Why does the skin look thick and scaly? | *Skin cells grow too fast and build up | It has been scratched by a cat | Too much sunshine | Cold weather has dried it out
exercise: Can you catch psoriasis by touching someone who has it? | Yes, from sharing a towel | Yes, if you touch the patches | *No, it is not catching | Only in the summer
test: Which parts of the body is psoriasis often found on? | Eyes and nose | Hands and feet | Back and tummy only | *Elbows, knees and scalp
test: Why do doctors give creams and other treatments for psoriasis? | They make skin cells grow faster | They cure it in one day | *They help, but the condition can come and go | They stop you catching it from others
exam: A classmate has psoriasis. What is the best thing to do? | *Be kind, because it is not catching and they may feel self-conscious | Ask them to hide their patches | Tell others they are dirty | Keep away from them in case you catch it
